# probes-suite-crashes-bricscad — the probes/ suite dies on BricsCAD before any record

STATUS: OPEN. Found 2026-08-30, the first time the probes/ suite was ever
pointed at BricsCAD.

## Symptom

On BOTH platforms the results file contains exactly one line:

    ((KIND . "wrapper-start"))

and nothing else. The wrapper opened the file, wrote its marker, and the
engine died before a single probe record — before even the run-start
that CAD-PROBE-BEGIN-RUN writes.

    macOS   (BricsCAD V26)      Segmentation fault: 11, runner exit 139
    Windows (BricsCAD V25 fr_FR) silent exit, run-probes reports
                                 "the wrapper started but never reached
                                  its end record"

AutoCAD ran the same wrapper, the same six suites, to completion. So did
the clautolisp baseline.

## Why it had never been seen

There was no CI job for this suite on BricsCAD at all — only AutoCAD —
and on a macOS workstation `make probe` could not FIND BricsCAD
(issues/closed/probe-detect-cad-misses-macos-bricscad.issue), so it
silently ran the clautolisp baseline instead. Between them, the suite had
simply never met the engine.

## What is known and what is not

The wrapper LOADS all six suite files and only then runs them, so the
death is in a LOAD or in CAD-PROBE-BEGIN-RUN, not in a probe body. That
is as far as the evidence goes. In particular it is NOT yet known whether
the fatal file is one of the four original suites or probe-foreach-scope
(added the same day), because they all load before anything runs.

PROBE_SUITES (added with this ticket) is what settles it: it narrows the
wrapper to named suites, so each can be tried alone. The BricsCAD jobs
now run `PROBE_SUITES=foreach-scope`, which both answers the question
that is actually open and performs half the experiment.

## Next

  - If foreach-scope alone RUNS: the fatal suite is one of the original
    four; bisect them the same way.
  - If foreach-scope alone DIES: the new file is implicated and should be
    reduced until it does not.

Either way the full suite should be restored on BricsCAD, and the
narrowing in .gitlab/native.yml removed, once the cause is known.

## The general point, again

`make probe` falls back to the clautolisp baseline when it detects no
CAD, and a suite that dies leaves a file behind. Both are good designs —
and both mean a run can SUCCEED at the shell level while measuring
nothing. The results themselves say which product answered; nothing else
does.

## CORRECTION (2026-08-31): the new suite was NOT exonerated. It was the cause.

The section below concluded, from a narrowed run that recorded nothing,
that probe-foreach-scope.lsp was innocent. That conclusion was WRONG, and
it was wrong because it read a SILENCE as evidence: the run produced only
`wrapper-start', so nothing said which file had died -- and I took
"rtos recorded nothing either" to mean rtos had been reached, when in
fact nothing had been reported at all.

Instrumenting every load settled it. On BricsCAD, Windows V25:

    ((KIND . "wrapper-start"))
    ((KIND . "load-context") (SECURELOAD . "0"))
    ((KIND . "load") (FILE . "probe-core.lsp") (VALUE . "nil"))
    ((KIND . "step") (AT . "loaded probe-rtos.lsp")
                     (VALUE . "CAD-PROBE-RUN-RTOS-PROBES"))

probe-rtos loads and DEFINES its function. The next marker, for
probe-foreach-scope.lsp, never appears. BricsCAD dies loading MY file.

THE FORM: `(foreach e (list 1 2 3))' -- a FOREACH WITH NO BODY, inside
CAD-PROBE-FOREACH--RETURN-VALUE. AutoCAD accepts it; clautolisp accepts
it; BricsCAD will not load a file containing it, and takes the other five
cases with it.

THE FIX, and the rule worth keeping: SYNTAX A HOST MIGHT REJECT GOES IN A
STRING, read at run time. A probe asks whether an engine accepts
something; writing the questionable form directly means the engine
answers by refusing to read the file, which is not an answer but a
silence. Held as text and READ when the case runs, a refusal becomes a
recorded `error' status for that one case and the suite continues.

SECURELOAD, incidentally, was cleared all along (0 in the record above),
so that suspicion -- also mine -- was wrong too.

## The experiment (2026-08-30): the new suite is EXONERATED [SUPERSEDED]

Two narrowed runs on the macOS runner, BricsCAD V26:

    PROBE_SUITES=foreach-scope        -> wrapper-start only, segfault
    PROBE_SUITES='rtos foreach-scope' -> wrapper-start only, segfault

RTOS is one of the four ORIGINAL suites and runs FIRST. It recorded
nothing either. So the engine dies before any pre-existing suite produces
a record, and probe-foreach-scope.lsp -- added the same day and the
obvious suspect -- is not the cause.

Narrowed further by what DID get written: `wrapper-start' is written by
the generated wrapper itself, after it opens the results file and before
it loads anything. So the wrapper ran, and death follows at
`(load probe-core.lsp)' or immediately after it. Nothing in the job log
distinguishes further: BricsCAD's -B output is not captured, so the
wrapper's own `cad-probe: loading wrapper' markers never appear.

The run takes ~13 seconds before the segfault, so BricsCAD is doing
something, not failing to start.

## Leading hypothesis: no drawing

The probes invoke

    "<bricscad>" -B <script.scr>

with NO DRAWING. Every BricsCAD job in this repository that WORKS passes
one -- vendor:probes, pathname:probe, encoding:experiment all set
PROBE_DWG (c:/gitlab-runner/dwg/empty.dwg on Windows) -- and probes/
README.org itself says to run "on a freshly launched CAD with a new empty
drawing".

A batch BricsCAD with no document may have no database to answer against,
and crash rather than refuse. That is testable: give the runner template
a drawing. It needs an empty .dwg on the macOS runner (the Windows one
already has one at the path above), so it is a runner-side prerequisite
rather than a code change alone.

AutoCAD's accoreconsole took the same wrapper with no drawing and ran it
to completion, which does not disprove the hypothesis -- accoreconsole is
built for exactly that -- but does mean the wrapper itself is sound.
